The real-world probability equation is even better

In most real projects, success has multiple stages:

\text{Success rate} = \text{Opportunity rate}\times\text{Detection rate}\times\text{Conversion rate}

For example, a business might have a valuable opportunity appear but fail to notice it. Or notice it but fail to convert it.

This reveals three places to improve:

* Opportunity generation: Find more qualified opportunities.
* Detection: Notice a larger proportion of the opportunities that already exist.
* Conversion: Successfully act on more of the opportunities you’ve noticed.

A shiny hunter optimizing encounters per hour is analogous to a business increasing qualified leads per week. But a shiny hunter finding a better odds-boosting method is analogous to a business improving how often qualified leads convert.

And a hunter discovering the Pokémon is shiny-locked is like discovering an opportunity was impossible to close under the existing conditions.

The best optimization can be deciding not to keep hunting the wrong target.

